WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.640
China's exports rose 25% last month, pushing its annual trade surplus past $800 billion, and shifting

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:16.000
pressure on global supply chains. This is Omni View, episode 169. Nikkei Asia, working from China's

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.760
official customs tables, lays out the August comparison with a year earlier as a sign that Chinese

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.240
factories are still holding volume, even while demand and other economies has cooled. The same monthly

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.700
breakdown shows goods leaving the country still advancing across a range of categories,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:38.640
not a single product line. That reporting also notes the surplus has been widening steadily

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.320
through the year, rather than appearing as a one-off bulge. Coverage of similar trade data has

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.940
put a different stress on the same tables. A large surplus can sharpen friction with partners who

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.840
read it as proof that market access is uneven. Those partners are not arguing that the shipments

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.700
did not happen. They are arguing about what the imbalance says about how open each market is to

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:07.460
the other. The figures themselves are China's customs statistics, the official count of goods

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.920
leaving and entering the country every month. Before those tables are published, they go through

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:19.260
standard checks on how cargo is valued and when it is timed. Mint on-hlying, the senior general

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:25.220
who leads Myanmar, traveled to Vietnam for talks on security cooperation and economic ties.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:31.620
Officials also took up how the shared border is run and how cargo moves along the trade corridors

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.940
that link the two countries. Hanoi is keeping a work in relationship with Myanmar's military

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:43.160
government, even though that government draws criticism abroad. Nikkeh Asia treats the visit as

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:48.520
of a piece with Vietnam's long habit of talking to neighbors in order to protect its own frontier

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:54.220
and its own commercial routes. Vietnam has not joined the sanctions that Western governments have

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.240
placed on Myanmar. Other regional reporting has stressed a more practical motive. These contacts

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:06.360
led Vietnam deal with border stability and refugee flows without waiting for a political turn inside

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.560
Myanmar. Vietnamese statements during the meetings put the emphasis on economic interests both countries

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:18.060
share. Everything in the metals dispute turns on whether Ottawa is restoring leverage or

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:23.990
straining a factory system the two countries built together. Cannaba has raised tariffs on selected

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:30.810
United States steel and aluminum goods to 50 percent twice the prior rate in a package CNBC

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:37.970
values at $27.6 billion. The duties apply to specific product lines coming in from the United

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.930
States and they followed earlier American tariffs in a fight that was already underway.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:48.610
They took effect on the day they were announced. Canadian officials call the step a reply to

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:54.930
American measures that in Ottawa's account hurt Canadian producers. They say the higher duties are

00:02:54.930 --> 00:03:00.050
needed to protect domestic plants and to keep bargaining power on the table and they present the

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:05.870
package as measured payback rather than a widening of the fight. In that telling doubling the rates

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:11.690
defends jobs in important manufacturing regions and resets the balance after American tariffs

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:18.350
lifted costs for Canadian exporters. The duties they add can come off once talks produce fairer

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:24.130
terms. United States trade representatives argue that the original American tariffs answer real

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:30.070
over capacity and subsidized output abroad and that they shield against unfair foreign competition.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:35.910
They warn that Canadian retaliation can damage tightly linked North American supply chains

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:42.450
without fixing the pricing distortions underneath. Saudi Arabia stopped work at several energy sites in

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:48.610
the South after attacks set fires in areas that sit along the Yemen border. Authorities said the

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:54.210
shutdowns are temporary while crews judge the damage. Bloomberg reports the pauses as a reaction

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:58.790
to recent strikes that have fed worry about how well energy infrastructure is protected.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.790
Saudi statements say the sites were made secure quickly and that any change in output is meant to

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:10.230
be short lived. Regional reporting has tied the incidents to a longer pattern of tension on that

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:16.090
frontier where cross-border strikes have flared from time to time. The plants involved are in the

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:22.710
kingdom's southern provinces and damage assessments are still underway. Cargo operators in Japan have

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:28.590
started trying sales and kites on freighters hoping the wind will cut how much fuel the engines burn.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:35.290
Nikkeh Asia says the tests answer higher bunker prices and growing international pressure to

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:40.530
bring down emissions from ships at sea. The equipment is meant to work beside the ordinary

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:47.070
motors not to replace them. Companies are fitting the systems onto hulls already in service rather

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:52.410
than waiting for a new generation of ships to be built. People who follow the industry note that

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:58.310
even a modest drop in fuel use can add up on long passages where the wind is fairly predictable.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:05.150
The work sits inside a wider push to hit efficiency targets that now apply across global shipping.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:11.630
Fermus, an Australian company, has signed on to deliver computing capacity that will run inside

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:19.210
data centers in Malaysia under a contract that includes open AI. Nikkeh Asia places the agreement inside

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:25.190
a surge in demand for data center space tied to artificial intelligence or AI workloads.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:31.150
The arrangement connects Australian kit with Malaysian hulls and it is aimed at the need for

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:37.230
compute at a very large scale. Firms are putting that kind of capacity where power and land are

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:42.670
cheap enough to support big hulls and Malaysian authorities have been courting those investments to

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:48.730
grow the country's technology sector. The partnership sets specific capacity commitments for the Malaysian

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:55.370
sites. In a piece of progress worth knowing, Nigeria's Ogonealand wetlands have been listed as a

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:01.270
wetland of international importance under the Ramsar Convention, the treaty that flags sites of

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:07.350
ecological value around the world. This day reports that a certificate was handed over after years

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:13.030
spent documenting what the marsh actually holds. The listing covers ground that people have long

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:18.790
used for fishing and farming. The designation gives federal and state authorities a shared frame

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:24.790
for conservation work. Communities in the area have pressed for stronger protection for a long time.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:31.330
The title does not by itself unlock new money, but it can help draw international backing toward

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:37.330
restoration that is already in motion. The ceremony marked the site's formal addition to the global

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:43.130
wetlands list. To really understand a trade surplus headline, you have to follow the paperwork

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:49.610
under it, not the adjective on the screen. A common mistake is to treat one month's export total

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:55.050
as a clean snapshot of economic strength when the published number is the end of a long chain.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:07:02.070
Customs offices first gather detailed invoices from each shipment, statisticians then convert those

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:08.130
invoices at the going exchange rate and strip out re-exports, goods that merely passed through

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:14.250
before they add the country total. Weeks or months later, late paperwork arrives and valuation

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:20.010
mistakes get fixed, so a headline that looked firm can read differently in the next quarter's tables.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.830
Seasonal adjustments then smooth holiday and harvest swings that would otherwise make one month

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:32.110
look wildly unlike the next. A sharp year on year jump can therefore owe something to when cargo

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:38.470
cleared the dock, not only to a lasting change in orders. When a minister or a commentator waves

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:44.590
the latest surplus as proof of resilience or of imbalance, it helps to ask whether the print is

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:50.590
still preliminary and whether earlier months were revised up or down. Cross-check the figure

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:56.030
against the original customs release rather than a second hand round up, and when you can,

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:01.270
set the same months count from the exporting country beside the count from the importing country.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.890
The footnotes on method often explain why those two numbers refuse to match.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:12.790
If you want to go deeper on the trade figures, compare how Nique, Asia and CNBC are covering them.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:19.190
You will see two different frames on the same facts. Tomorrow, watch for any update on those Saudi

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:24.930
damage assessments, and for any signal that Ottawa and Washington are opening talks on the metals

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:31.910
duties. That's omniview. As always, compare outlets, look for primary documents, and separate

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:37.210
what's known from what's assumed. The world is complicated, but it's not beyond understanding,

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:42.390
and you just spent a few minutes understanding it better. If that's worth something to you,

